"The postoperative pathology reveals a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) diagnosis with pathological stage pT2. A key adverse finding is positive surgical margins, which is a significant prognostic factor. Counterbalancing this is the absence of extraprostatic extension and the finding that the seminal vesicles were not invaded. Lymphovascular invasion was absent. Nodal status is documented as pNx, meaning no lymph nodes were removed, which widens uncertainty regarding nodal involvement. Biopsy findings showed Gleason 3+3 and 3+4, with perineural invasion noted in one sample. Postoperative PSA is not recorded, so biochemical recurrence cannot be assessed. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its trajectory is crucial for revising the prognosis. Missing nodal sampling limits staging and does not establish node-negative disease. The fixed estimated horizon is 47.46 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 28.47–66.44 months; its coverage has not been validated."
